Z||000008||DecadenceR||06-07-2001||12:19 PM||||That, my good man, is "Rice Syndrome" in it's most concentrated... and deadly form.<P>You see, typical Rice Syndrome, or RSD is an airborne pathogen that can attack the brain and central nervous system. This can sometimes be cured by antibiotics, a short hospital stay, sometimes a new car. The disease can be killed with simple disinfectsnts in the surrounding area.<BR>However, Mad Honda disease, or MHD, is much much harder to cure. It has a very high tolerance to any conventional treatments. It is also usually charatcerized by denial over losing races to any american car. This strain of the disease is HIGHLY contageous, and may only be destroyed by extremely high levels of heat or radistion.||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||06-07-2001||12:46 PM||||I have heard that there is no real easy cure for Mad Honda Diasese (MHD). Once contracted it can lead to other nasty little bugs such as Rice Syndrom (RD). There were some instances of this as posted by several (unnamed) persons on this forum. I remember on post on a Fiero with wheels all splayed out from under the fenders! IT CAN HAPPEN! Early signs of RD are wide wheels that stick out beyond the fenders, 12 inch rims, and the chrome coffee can stuck on the tail pipe! <BR>MHD in pure form (a Honda for example)can only be cured by a crusher! or numerous beatings at stop light grand Prix's that will eventualy wear down the owner of said infected car. A Fiero with MHD needs MUCH reworking to cure.<BR>There has been a recent mutation to this called Raised Wing Syndrome (RWS), Please be weary of this mutation as it spreading at an alarming rate!!<P>BTW <BR>It is believed that a toyota corolla back in the 70's is believed to be "patient zero" when the first chrome tailpipe was installed on it||||reg||6
